    {"track": "finance", "title": "Market-Linked vs Fixed-Return Investments (General Concept)", "difficulty": "medium", "points_value": 80, "deliverable_type": "text", "requires_geotag": False, "estimated_duration": "3-4 hours",
     "brief": "Two common ways people grow savings are market-linked investments and fixed-return instruments. Understand the general trade-off as a finance concept anyone should know — not tied to any specific company or product.",
     "why_it_matters": "This task builds the kind of financial literacy that helps you manage money at work AND in your own life. This is exactly the kind of skill employers look for, regardless of which company or industry you end up joining.",
     "instructions": "Step 1: Research the general difference between a market-linked investment (returns can go up or down over time) and a fixed-return instrument (a set, guaranteed return, usually lower).\nStep 2: List one advantage and one risk of each type, in general terms.\nStep 3: In 2-3 lines, explain why someone might choose to split their savings across both types instead of putting everything into just one.\nStep 4: Don't stop at the first example — dig deeper. Research or work through at least 3-4 real examples/cases related to \"Market-Linked vs Fixed-Return Investments (General Concept)\", not just one, before you move on.\nStep 5: Now write it up properly, in your own words — a clear, well-organised summary of at least 400 words covering everything you found, structured like you're presenting it to a manager (not just a few bullet points).\nStep 6: Finally, write a short reflection (150+ words) — what surprised you while working on this, what was harder than it looked, and how this exact skill would be useful at ANY company you might work at, not just this one."},
    {"track": "finance", "title": "Understanding Net Worth Calculation", "difficulty": "easy", "points_value": 60, "deliverable_type": "text", "requires_geotag": False, "estimated_duration": "2.5-3.5 hours",
     "brief": "Net worth is a simple snapshot of someone's overall financial position. Learn to calculate it.",
     "why_it_matters": "This task teaches you to back decisions with numbers instead of guesswork, which every employer notices. This is exactly the kind of skill employers look for, regardless of which company or industry you end up joining.",
     "instructions": "Step 1: Learn the formula: Net Worth = Total Assets (what you own — cash, property, investments) minus Total Liabilities (what you owe — loans, credit card debt).\nStep 2: Create a hypothetical example with at least 3 assets and 2 liabilities, and calculate the net worth.\nStep 3: In 2 lines, explain why tracking net worth over time is more useful than looking at income alone.\nStep 4: Don't stop at the first example — dig deeper. Research or work through at least 3-4 real examples/cases related to \"Understanding Net Worth Calculation\", not just one, before you move on.\nStep 5: Now write it up properly, in your own words — a clear, well-organised summary of at least 400 words covering everything you found, structured like you're presenting it to a manager (not just a few bullet points).\nStep 6: Finally, write a short reflection (150+ words) — what surprised you while working on this, what was harder than it looked, and how this exact skill would be useful at ANY company you might work at, not just this one."},

